    "body": "Copper cathodes at Gunnison's Johnson Camp mine processing facility in Arizona. Credit: Gunnison Copper via Facebook.\nPosted By: Northern Miner Staff November 18, 2026\nCopper fell to a seven-month low in London on Wednesday as a stronger dollar and doubts about spending on artificial intelligence weighed on prices even as Chinese smelters face the prospect of paying miners to keep their furnaces fed.\nThree-month copper touched $12,700 a tonne and traded at $12,770, down 3.5%, at 6 a.m. Toronto time, Reuters reported. In Shanghai, consultancy CRU expects the 2027 annual treatment charge to settle between minus $50 and minus $100 per tonne of copper concentrate, BMO Capital Markets commodities analysts Helen Amos and George Heppel wrote Wednesday from the World Copper Conference.\nEven at the same headline fee, “the reality for smelter economics will vary heavily depending on acid price arrangements, metal payables terms and other commercial terms,” the BMO analysts wrote.\nTreatment charges\nThe two prices measure different things. London futures track refined copper, while the treatment charge is what a miner normally pays a smelter to process concentrate. When smelters compete for scarce feed, the charge can turn negative, giving the miner a premium. A smelter can offset some of the loss by selling sulphuric acid, a by-product, although acid prices have fallen since July, BMO said.\nThe squeeze is already more severe for smelters buying cargoes on the spot market. Trader-to-smelter treatment charges are below minus $200 per tonne of concentrate. CRU estimates that Chinese smelters’ average cash margin has fallen to nearly minus $200 a tonne from around break-even in July, according to BMO. Chilean miner Antofagasta (LSE: ANTO) is negotiating next year’s terms with Chinese smelters.\nBuyers of refined metal in China are also paying more for prompt delivery. The Yangshan premium, paid on top of the London price for imported copper, reached $350 a tonne on Tuesday, up from $290 the day before, Reuters reported. CRU still expects a global refined surplus of about 200,000 tonnes in 2026 and 2027, BMO said. Print\nTAG: copper copper price copper production\nCOMMODITY: Copper\nCOMPANY: Antofagasta\nRepublish this article",
